    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65214",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression ${cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{3}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{3}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{3}-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}⁡x}}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)={sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
