    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65217",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{sin}^{3}⁡x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{sin}^{3}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\frac{3{sin}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}-\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =3-3{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x$  to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{sin}^{3}⁡x)=3{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$",
