    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65211",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$",
