      "problem": "$\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the definition of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡A$ to rewrite the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-{tan}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $1-{sin}^{2}⁡A={cos}^{2}⁡A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=1\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
