    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65236",
      "knowledge_point_id": "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$",
