    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-119869",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "If $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{2\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$",
