      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3175",
      "knowledge_point_id": "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3176",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
